## Doc-state sugar

An anchor <sec_flow> and a reference &sec_flow resolve; a kebab anchor <sec-kebab>
and its reference &sec-kebab resolve too. A footnote fires after punctuation.&n1 A
repeat shares its number,&n1 plus a second.&n2 A custom-text reference
&sec_flow{this very section} reads naturally. Trailing punctuation stays literal:
&sec_flow. keeps the dot. Literal Vec<T> and R&D stay text; see &sec_flow[1] keeps
its bracket prose.

@Footnote[id: "n1"]: The first footnote body, with @em{markup}.

  A second paragraph continues the first note, so the entry decodes as flow.
@Footnote[id: "n2"]: The second footnote body.

## Doc-state constructs

% secset({ numberDepth: 2 })
% bibset({ src: { knuth84: { author: "Knuth", title: "The TeXbook", year: 1984 } } })

A table of contents lists the headings above:

@Toc{}

Knuth's classic @Cite{knuth84} anchors a bibliography; a bare key ref cites with a
locator &knuth84[page: "33"] through the same entry. An anonymous
@Footnote{a one-shot note} sits beside a labeled one.&n3 That one is defined by the
element colon form:

@Footnote[id: "n3"]: A third footnote body, via the element colon form.

@aside[class: "x"]: A styled colon-body aside; props compose with the colon.

